{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And he made the sea dry land, and the waters were split.</b> It should have said first 'and the waters were split' and afterwards 'and he made the sea dry land.' And perhaps to settle this, Rashi explained 'and the waters were split' — all the waters in the world. And it appears possible to explain that at first the wind that was within the earth acted from beneath the earth in the sea-bed until it became dry land, and afterwards it acted also upon the waters, for the action of the wind was from below upward, and when it passed through the sea-bed the wind entered also into the waters and the wind diminished them and reduced them, for it is the nature of wind to diminish water that has congealed and become ice, and then inevitably the waters split and what remained stood up like a wall, for they had crusted over because of the wind that rose from below — for anything moist that dries from within splits, as is known from natural phenomena." "Another matter: the wind carried the water of the sea from the dry ground toward the land, and the waters went out from their banks toward the land and likewise from the other side, and this caused the splitting of the waters, for the wind drove the waters from their place and carried them to the land. And this is a twofold miracle, for this matter requires two opposing winds, yet all was accomplished by means of an east wind. Another matter: because had the deep not kept adding moisture to the waters, they would have gone on diminishing from the force of the wind that prevails over them. And when he made the sea dry land first, so that the deep no longer added moisture to them, then inevitably the waters dried up and were diminished by the wind."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:12.197581611Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}